- How would you adapt for arbitrary precision (128-bit) using two limbs?
- How would you detect when no next permutation exists efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to scale classic SWAR nibble-swaps to 128-bit values, ensuring implementations stay constant-time and border-safe.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane-wise bit-twiddling
- SWAR-style mask-and-shift patterns
- Safe handling of hi/lo halves

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 nibbleSwap128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & M) << 4) | ((x.lo >> 4) & M);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & M) << 4) | ((x.hi >> 4) & M);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 256-bit types? 
- How do you test with all-zero and all-0xFF inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

- Demonstrates ability to replace branches with bit-twiddling for performance.
- Applies a well-known, portable technique to a common memory-allocation problem.
- Handles edge cases and overflow without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix-fill trick to propagate the highest set bit.
- Branchless arithmetic to compute next power of two.
- Overflow behavior on 32-bit boundaries.

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t nextPowerOfTwo32(uint32_t n) {
  // Assumes n > 0. No explicit branches.
  n--;
  n |= n >> 1;
  n |= n >> 2;
  n |= n >> 4;
  n |= n >> 8;
  n |= n >> 16;
  return n + 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this behave for n = 0x80000000 or 0xFFFFFFFF?
- Can you adapt this to 64-bit inputs with no loops?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a deterministic, branchless 128-bit bit-permutation using fixed masks, a common pattern in high-performance bit-twiddling tasks relevant to databases and graphics.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit word with two 64-bit halves
- Nibble-wise bit permutation via fixed masks
- Branchless, mask-and-shift composition

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 galacticShuffle128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M0 = 0x1111111111111111ULL;
  const uint64_t M1 = 0x2222222222222222ULL;
  const uint64_t M2 = 0x4444444444444444ULL;
  const uint64_t M3 = 0x8888888888888888ULL;

  uint64_t lo0 = x.lo & M0;
  uint64_t lo1 = x.lo & M1;
  uint64_t lo2 = x.lo & M2;
  uint64_t lo3 = x.lo & M3;

  uint64_t hi0 = x.hi & M0;
  uint64_t hi1 = x.hi & M1;
  uint64_t hi2 = x.hi & M2;
  uint64_t hi3 = x.hi & M3;

  uint128 r;
  r.lo = lo0 | (lo2 >> 1) | (lo1 << 1) | lo3;
  r.hi = hi0 | (hi2 >> 1) | (hi1 << 1) | hi3;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to a 256-bit type with the same nibble-permutation rule?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures (SIMD lanes, interleaving with memory)?
